{"content_hash": "d0decebb2eb64e15", "version_id": 277, "article_id": 28, "level_code": "ja_n4", "lang": "ja", "level": 2, "level_label": "N4 初級", "title": "世界でけがが大きな問題に 病院のつくり方を見直すとき", "title_zh": "全球受伤问题严重，医院体系该改改了", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-global-injury-crisis-highlights-urgent.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49492358448_70ce40b0d5_b.jpg", "credit": "NavyMedicine · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 479, "reading_minutes": 1.5, "lead": "世界では毎年、多くの人がけがで亡くなったり、体が不自由になったりしています。新しい研究が、病院の使い方をもっとよくする必要があると話しています。", "lead_zh": "每年全世界都有很多人因为受伤而死亡或落下残疾。一项新研究指出，我们需要更好地改进医院的服务方式。", "preview": "イギリスのバーミンガム大学などの研究チームが、アフリカと南アジアの4か国で調べました。ガーナ、パキスタン、ルワンダ、南アフリカの19の病院で、けがをした8858人の患者を3か月間追いました。すると、47.6%の人が亡くなるか、中等度から重度の障害が残りました。", "preview_zh": "英国伯明翰大学等机构的研究团队在非洲和南亚的4个国家进行了调查。他们追踪了加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非19家医院中8858名受伤患者3个月。结果，47.6%的人死亡或留下中度到重度残疾。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 267, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@cec1515/content/audio/28/ja_n4.mp3", "duration": 101.85, "size_bytes": 614880, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 325, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@cec1515/content/data/versions/277.json", "paragraphs": ["イギリスのバーミンガム大学などの研究チームが、アフリカと南アジアの4か国で調べました。ガーナ、パキスタン、ルワンダ、南アフリカの19の病院で、けがをした8858人の患者を3か月間追いました。すると、47.6%の人が亡くなるか、中等度から重度の障害が残りました。", "研究チームは、けがそのものよりも、患者がどのように病院へ行くかが大切だと分かりました。救急車で来た患者は、他の方法で来た患者より、亡くなるか障害が残る割合が86%も高かったのです。これは救急車が悪いという意味ではありません。救急の仕組み全体がうまく連携していないと、効果が出にくいということです。", "また、早く病院に着いても、亡くなる危険は下がりませんでした。少し遅れた患者のほうが、後で障害が残りにくいことも分かりました。年をとっていると結果は悪くなりましたが、性別やお金、教育は入院中の生存にあまり関係しませんでした。しかし退院後は、お金があって教育が高い人のほうがよく回復しました。", "けがによる死亡の90%は低・中所得国で起きています。しかし、世界の保健政策では、感染症や慢性の病気ほど注目されていません。研究チームは、それぞれの国の状況に合ったけがの治療体制をつくるべきだと言っています。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["英国伯明翰大学等机构的研究团队在非洲和南亚的4个国家进行了调查。他们追踪了加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非19家医院中8858名受伤患者3个月。结果，47.6%的人死亡或留下中度到重度残疾。", "研究团队发现，比起受伤本身，患者如何去医院更重要。坐救护车来的患者，死亡或留下残疾的比例比用其他方式来的患者高出86%。这并不是说救护车不好，而是说如果整个急救体系没有很好地配合，就很难有效果。", "另外，即使很快到达医院，死亡风险也没有降低。研究还发现，稍微晚一点到的患者，之后反而不容易留下残疾。年纪大的人结果更差，但性别、金钱和教育程度与住院期间的生存关系不大。不过出院后，有钱、教育程度高的人恢复得更好。", "因受伤死亡的人中，90%发生在中低收入国家。但在全球卫生政策中，它受到的关注不如传染病和慢性病。研究团队表示，应该建立适合各国国情的创伤治疗体系。"], "body": "イギリスのバーミンガム大学などの研究チームが、アフリカと南アジアの4か国で調べました。ガーナ、パキスタン、ルワンダ、南アフリカの19の病院で、けがをした8858人の患者を3か月間追いました。すると、47.6%の人が亡くなるか、中等度から重度の障害が残りました。\n\n研究チームは、けがそのものよりも、患者がどのように病院へ行くかが大切だと分かりました。救急車で来た患者は、他の方法で来た患者より、亡くなるか障害が残る割合が86%も高かったのです。これは救急車が悪いという意味ではありません。救急の仕組み全体がうまく連携していないと、効果が出にくいということです。\n\nまた、早く病院に着いても、亡くなる危険は下がりませんでした。少し遅れた患者のほうが、後で障害が残りにくいことも分かりました。年をとっていると結果は悪くなりましたが、性別やお金、教育は入院中の生存にあまり関係しませんでした。しかし退院後は、お金があって教育が高い人のほうがよく回復しました。\n\nけがによる死亡の90%は低・中所得国で起きています。しかし、世界の保健政策では、感染症や慢性の病気ほど注目されていません。研究チームは、それぞれの国の状況に合ったけがの治療体制をつくるべきだと言っています。", "body_zh": "英国伯明翰大学等机构的研究团队在非洲和南亚的4个国家进行了调查。他们追踪了加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非19家医院中8858名受伤患者3个月。结果，47.6%的人死亡或留下中度到重度残疾。\n\n研究团队发现，比起受伤本身，患者如何去医院更重要。坐救护车来的患者，死亡或留下残疾的比例比用其他方式来的患者高出86%。这并不是说救护车不好，而是说如果整个急救体系没有很好地配合，就很难有效果。\n\n另外，即使很快到达医院，死亡风险也没有降低。研究还发现，稍微晚一点到的患者，之后反而不容易留下残疾。年纪大的人结果更差，但性别、金钱和教育程度与住院期间的生存关系不大。不过出院后，有钱、教育程度高的人恢复得更好。\n\n因受伤死亡的人中，90%发生在中低收入国家。但在全球卫生政策中，它受到的关注不如传染病和慢性病。研究团队表示，应该建立适合各国国情的创伤治疗体系。", "text": "イギリスのバーミンガム大学などの研究チームが、アフリカと南アジアの4か国で調べました。ガーナ、パキスタン、ルワンダ、南アフリカの19の病院で、けがをした8858人の患者を3か月間追いました。すると、47.6%の人が亡くなるか、中等度から重度の障害が残りました。 研究チームは、けがそのものよりも、患者がどのように病院へ行くかが大切だと分かりました。救急車で来た患者は、他の方法で来た患者より、亡くなるか障害が残る割合が86%も高かったのです。これは救急車が悪いという意味ではありません。救急の仕組み全体がうまく連携していないと、効果が出にくいということです。 また、早く病院に着いても、亡くなる危険は下がりませんでした。少し遅れた患者のほうが、後で障害が残りにくいことも分かりました。年をとっていると結果は悪くなりましたが、性別やお金、教育は入院中の生存にあまり関係しませんでした。しかし退院後は、お金があって教育が高い人のほうがよく回復しました。 けがによる死亡の90%は低・中所得国で起きています。しかし、世界の保健政策では、感染症や慢性の病気ほど注目されていません。研究チームは、それぞれの国の状況に合ったけがの治療体制をつくるべきだと言っています。", "vocab": [{"word": "けが", "pos": "noun", "zh": "受伤", "note": "日常常用词，汉字可写“怪我”"}, {"word": "患者", "pos": "noun", "zh": "患者", "note": "医院里看病的人"}, {"word": "病院", "pos": "noun", "zh": "医院", "note": "比“医院”更常用"}, {"word": "研究", "pos": "noun", "zh": "研究", "note": "する动词也常用"}, {"word": "障害", "pos": "noun", "zh": "残疾，障碍", "note": "身体不方便的状态"}, {"word": "救急車", "pos": "noun", "zh": "救护车", "note": "紧急时叫的车"}, {"word": "亡くなる", "pos": "verb", "zh": "去世", "note": "比“死ぬ”更礼貌"}, {"word": "回復", "pos": "noun", "zh": "恢复", "note": "身体变好"}, {"word": "教育", "pos": "noun", "zh": "教育", "note": "学校学习"}, {"word": "政策", "pos": "noun", "zh": "政策", "note": "国家或政府的方针"}], "title_original": "Global injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care", "summary_original": "A study of almost 9,000 injury patients across Africa and South Asia has found that nearly half were dead or disabled after three months—highlighting the need for smarter, locally tailored trauma-care systems.", "body_original": "Global injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care\nby\nUniversity of Birmingham\nedited by\nSadie Harley\n, reviewed by\nAndrew Zinin\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\npeer-reviewed publication\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nA study of almost 9,000 injury patients across Africa and South Asia has found that nearly half were dead or disabled after three months—highlighting the need for smarter, locally tailored trauma-care systems.\nPublishing its\nfindings in\nThe Lancet Global Health\n, the international research group led by University of Birmingham experts reveals that the biggest influences on survival and recovery are not just the injuries themselves, but how people move through the health care system.\nInjuries cause an estimated 4.4 million deaths annually worldwide, with 90% occurring in LMICs. This is the first large multicountry study to examine mortality and disability outcomes among hospitalized injury patients across low- or middle-income countries (LMIC).\nThe study reveals that access to appropriate care, the route patients take to the hospital and the type of services available all play an important role in these outcomes.\nAnalyzing data from 8,858 patients treated in 19 urban and rural hospitals across Ghana, Pakistan, Rwanda and South Africa, the Global Health Group on Equitable Access to Quality Health Care for Injured People in Four Low- or Middle-Income Countries (Equi-injury) reveals an enormous and often overlooked global health burden:\n47.6% of patients either died or were left with moderate-to-severe disability within three months of hospital discharge.\n6.1% died in the hospital, 9.7% had died within three months, and 40.6% were living with moderate-to-severe disability three months after discharge.\nPatients arriving at the hospital by ambulance had significantly worse outcomes, with 86% higher odds of death or disability and more than double the odds of mortality within three months compared with those using other forms of transportation.\nLead author Professor Justine Davies, from the University of Birmingham, said, \"One of our most surprising findings was that measures often assumed to improve outcomes—such as ambulance transport and rapid transfer to major hospitals, which are similar to highly centralized trauma services—were not consistently linked with better recovery or survival.\n\"This does not mean that ambulances cause worse outcomes but suggests that investment in ambulance systems alone is unlikely to improve outcomes unless services are coordinated and appropriately resourced.\"\nThe findings challenge assumptions that health care models developed in wealthier countries can simply be copied elsewhere. Instead, the study argues that countries should develop trauma and injury-care systems based on local evidence, resources and patient needs.\nCorresponding author Dr. Leila Ghalichi, from the University of Birmingham, said, \"This does not mean ambulances or specialist trauma centers are ineffective. Rather, it suggests that simply investing in expensive emergency systems is not enough.\n\"LMICs should be cautious about investing heavily in ambulance services and specialist, centralized trauma centers without ensuring they are well coordinated, properly staffed and supported by strong health systems. Better data and further research are needed to understand which trauma-care investments improve patient outcomes.\"\nThe study notes that stopping first at a nearby hospital for emergency stabilization may sometimes be beneficial, especially if a specialist hospital is far away. If a major trauma hospital is close by, going directly there may improve outcomes.\nResearchers found no evidence that reaching a hospital more quickly reduced the risk of death. Patients who experienced some delays were less likely to be disabled later—challenging the common belief that faster treatment always leads to better outcomes after injury.\nAge was linked to worse outcomes, but gender, wealth and education had little effect on survival while patients were in the hospital. However, once out of the hospital, wealthier and better-educated patients tended to recover better, suggesting that access to rehabilitation and follow-up care may play an important role.\nSome 90% of global injury deaths occur in LMICs and are estimated to account for around 10% of the global burden of disease. Yet trauma care receives far less attention than infectious diseases or chronic conditions in global health policy.\nCountry-level differences were striking. Patients in South Africa had the lowest odds of disability but the highest odds of mortality, while patients in Rwanda experienced the lowest odds of mortality. Meanwhile, patients in Pakistan experienced substantially higher odds of the combined outcome of death or disability than those in Ghana.\nThe study population reflects the demographics of injury worldwide. The median age was 31 years, 76.8% were male, and road traffic collisions accounted for 50% of all injuries recorded. Orthopedic injuries were the most common injury type, affecting 39.2% of patients.\nPublication details\nJustine Davies et al, Mortality and disability after hospitalisation for injuries in low-income and middle-income countries: a cohort study,\nThe Lancet Global Health\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1016/j.langlo.2026.104053\nJournal information:\nThe Lancet Global Health\nKey medical concepts\nInjury\nMortality\nClinical categories\nEmergency medicine\nHospital medicine\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nProvided by\nUniversity of Birmingham\nWho's behind this story?\nSadie Harley\nBSc Life Sciences & Ecology. Microbiology lab background with pharmaceutical news experience in oil, gas, and renewable industries.\nFull profile →\nAndrew Zinin\nMaster's in physics with research experience. Long-time science news enthusiast. Plays key role in Science X's editorial success.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nGlobal injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-global-injury-crisis-highlights-urgent.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T12:35:25.451601+00:00", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "イギリス", "s": 0.163, "e": 0.625, "sm": 162, "em": 625, "cs": 0, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "の", "s": 0.637, "e": 0.775, "sm": 637, "em": 774, "cs": 4, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "バーミンガム", "s": 0.787, "e": 1.462, "sm": 787, "em": 1462, "cs": 5, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "大学", "s": 1.475, "e": 1.9, "sm": 1475, "em": 1900, "cs": 11, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "など", "s": 1.913, "e": 2.113, "sm": 1912, "em": 2112, "cs": 13, "ce": 15, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "の", "s": 2.125, "e"